.TH CSESS 1 2026-11-23 csess "User Commands"
.SH NAME
csess \- list, fork and move Claude Code sessions between directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B csess ls
.RB [ \-a | \-\-all ]
.RI [ dir ]
.br
.B csess fork
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-memory ]
.RI [ srcdir " | "
.B \-s
.IR id ]
.I dir
.br
.B csess mv
.RB [ \-n ]
.I olddir newdir
.br
.B csess completion
.BR bash | zsh | fish | powershell
.SH DESCRIPTION
Claude Code ties every session to the directory it was started in.
Sessions are stored as
.IP
.IR $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR /projects/ encoded-path / session-id .jsonl
.PP
plus an optional
.IR session-id /
directory holding subagent transcripts and tool results.
.I encoded-path
is the absolute path with every character other than
.BR a\-z ,
.B A\-Z
and
.B 0\-9
replaced by
.BR \- .
.B claude \-c
and
.B claude \-\-resume
only see the sessions of the current directory, so renaming a directory
"loses" its sessions.
.B csess
lists them, copies them to other directories, and moves them along when a
directory is renamed.
.PP
Because of the encoding, different directories can share a folder name:
.IR a/b\-c ,
.I a/b/c
and
.I a/b.c
all become
.IR \-a\-b\-c .
.B csess
tells such sessions apart by the working directory recorded inside each
transcript.
.PP
Options may appear before or after arguments.
Use
.B \-\-
before a directory name that starts with
.BR \- .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-color " auto\fR|\fBon\fR|\fBoff"
Color the output.
.B auto
(the default) colors each of standard output and standard error only when it
is a terminal, unless
.B NO_COLOR
is set or
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Help for
.B csess
or a command.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "ls [\fIdir\fP]"
List the sessions of
.I dir
(default: the current directory), newest first.
Columns are last modification time, session id and title.
The title is the name given with
.BR /rename ,
else Claude's automatic title, else the last prompt; blank titles are skipped.
.B *
marks the session
.B claude \-c
would resume.
A session that shares the folder but was started in another directory is
shown as
.BI "[from " dir ]\fR.
Titles are cut to the terminal width when writing to a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Instead, print one line per directory that has sessions: last activity,
number of sessions, path.
Directories that share a folder name get a line each.
.B MISSING
means the directory no longer exists (deleted, or renamed without
.BR "csess mv" );
recover with
.B csess mv
after recreating it, or with
.BR "csess fork \-s" " \fIid newdir\fP."
Folders holding only Claude's
.I memory/
are listed as
.BR "(memory only)" .
.SS "fork [\-l] [\-\-no\-memory] [\fIsrcdir\fP | \-s \fIid\fP] \fIdir\fP"
Copy a session into
.I dir
(created, with its parents, if needed) and print the command that resumes
the copy.
The session copied is the newest one started in
.I srcdir
(default: the current directory), or the one given with
.BR \-s .
Right after a fork,
.B claude \-c
in
.I dir
resumes the copy too, since it is the newest session there.
The copy gets a new session id, so it never collides with sessions already
in
.IR dir ;
the original session is left untouched.
Copied along:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
the transcript, its subagent transcripts and tool results;
.IP \(bu 2
the session's scratchpad
.RI ( /tmp/claude\- uid / folder / id /scratchpad );
.IP \(bu 2
the source directory's
.I memory/
(it belongs to the directory, not the session), unless
.B \-\-no\-memory
is given.
Symbolic links in it are followed, so the copy shares no files with the
source.
If
.I dir
already has memory with files that differ, the fork is refused: merge the two
by hand, or use
.BR \-\-no\-memory .
Identical memory is left as is.
.RE
.PP
In the copy's transcripts, the recorded working directory, the directories of
.B /rewind
backups, and the paths of tool results, scratchpad and memory files are
rewritten to the new locations, and the session id is replaced.
Paths inside Claude's own configuration directory are left alone.
Tool-result and scratchpad files are copied byte for byte.
.B /rewind
checkpoints themselves are not copied.
Everything is copied under temporary names first, so a failed or interrupted
fork leaves nothing behind; once the copy is complete it is kept, even if
starting Claude fails.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-session " \fIid\fP"
Session to fork, from any directory; a unique prefix of the id is enough.
Can't be combined with
.IR srcdir .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-launch
Start
.B claude \-\-resume
on the copy in
.I dir
instead of printing the command.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-memory
Don't copy the source directory's
.IR memory/ .
.SS "mv [\-n] \fIolddir newdir\fP"
Rename or move a directory together with everything Claude keys by its path.
In order:
.IP 1. 4
Move
.I olddir
to
.I newdir
(parent directories are created; moves across filesystems fall back to
.BR mv (1)).
.IP 2. 4
Move the sessions of
.I olddir
and of all its subdirectories to the folders Claude expects for their new
paths.
If a target folder already exists the sessions are merged into it, directory
by directory.
Files that would be overwritten, such as a second
.IR memory/MEMORY.md ,
are not: they are moved to
.IR projects/csess\-conflicts\- YYYYMMDD\-HHMMSS
and listed, to be merged by hand.
A folder holding only
.I memory/
whose name could also stand for a directory outside
.I olddir
is left in place, with a warning.
Sessions of other directories that share a folder name stay where they are.
All folders are staged under
.IR projects/.csess\-mv\- *
first, so a target name that equals another source's name is handled
correctly.
If staging or rewriting fails, everything, including the directory itself, is
put back.
.IP 3. 4
Rewrite the recorded working directories, the directories of
.B /rewind
backups, and tool-result paths inside the moved transcripts.
Modification times are kept, so
.B claude \-c
still resumes the same session.
.IP 4. 4
Update the up-arrow prompt history
.RI ( history.jsonl ).
.IP 5. 4
Rename the entries for
.I olddir
and its subdirectories in
.I .claude.json
(trust, allowed tools, local MCP servers, GitHub repository paths), keeping
the order of keys.
A symbolic link to
.I .claude.json
or
.I history.jsonl
is followed and stays a link.
A backup is written to
.IR .claude.json.csess\-bak. YYYYMMDD\-HHMMSS
when anything changes.
.PP
Before changing anything,
.B mv
checks that every folder it will write to is writable, every transcript is
readable, and
.I .claude.json
parses; otherwise it stops and nothing is changed.
It also refuses when
.I newdir
exists, when
.I newdir
is inside
.IR olddir ,
when
.I olddir
is a symbolic link (move the target, or rename the link with
.BR mv (1)),
when
.I olddir
contains Claude's own configuration, and when a
.B claude
process of the current user is running inside
.IR olddir .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show which folders would be moved; change nothing.
.PP
Not handled: Python virtual environments, git worktrees (run
.BR "git worktree repair" ),
and absolute paths inside your own files or
.IR .claude/settings.local.json .
Other running Claude sessions may rewrite
.I .claude.json
while
.B mv
runs; it is safest to run it with Claude closed.
.SS "completion bash|zsh|fish|powershell"
Print the tab-completion script for a shell.
Packages install these system-wide, so normally nothing needs to be done.
To load it by hand, for example:
.EX
source <(csess completion bash)
csess completion zsh > "${fpath[1]}/_csess"
csess completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/csess.fish
.EE
Run
.B csess completion \fIshell\fP \-\-help
for details.
Completions cover commands, options, directories and, for
.BR "fork \-\-session" ,
session ids with their titles.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, colors are off unless
.B \-\-color on
is given.
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
Claude's configuration directory (default:
.IR ~/.claude ).
When set,
.I .claude.json
is looked up inside it instead of in
.IR $HOME .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.claude/projects/
Session transcripts, one folder per directory.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/history.jsonl
Up-arrow prompt history.
.TP
.I ~/.claude.json
Per-project settings.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on any error.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Claude truncates folder names longer than 200 characters and appends a hash.
.B ls
and
.B fork
find such folders by their recorded working directory;
.B mv
refuses to create them, and leaves
.IR memory/ \-only
folders with hashed names in place, with a warning.
Paths containing newlines are not supported.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
csess ls
csess ls \-\-all | grep MISSING
csess fork ../experiment
csess fork ~/work/api ~/scratch/api\-try
csess fork \-l \-s 20cb319a ~/scratch/try2
csess mv \-\-dry\-run ~/work/app ~/work/app\-v2
csess mv ~/work/app ~/work/app\-v2
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR claude (1),
.BR mv (1)
